{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>Balak slaughtered, etc.</b> The verse intends here also to make known the disgrace of this man, that Balak did not consider him worthy to dine with him even at a first meal, but rather sent to him and to the ministers, etc. Or perhaps he was precise in saying <b>and to the ministers who were with him</b> — that he sent only enough for Balaam and for the known ministers who were with him and the baggage of his servants, and this reveals Balak's narrow-eyed stinginess toward honoring Balaam. Or it may be because Balak thought that Balaam would take all that was sent to him and keep it for himself alone and not give to the ministers, etc., for it was known that he was evil-eyed; therefore when he sent, he stated explicitly that the slaughtered meat sent would not be his alone but for him and for the ministers. And he said <b>who were with him</b> — perhaps he apportioned according to the count of the ministers who were with him, excluding an uninvited guest, so that if other ministers joined him there would be no portion of meat for them. And therefore you will find that our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 20:16) that Balaam was distressed over the meagerness of the meat and said, ״Tomorrow I am sending a curse upon this one's property,״ and the next day he said to him, ״Build for me, etc., seven bulls and seven rams״ (Numbers 23:1)."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:57.279919172Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}